{"id": 15355, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 9-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for evaluation of swelling around her eyes that developed over the past week.\u00a0 She has been taking ibuprofen daily for the last 3 months for various sports-related injuries and has no chronic medical conditions.\u00a0 Vital signs are normal.\u00a0 On physical examination, there is moderate periorbital edema with bilateral lower extremity pitting edema.\u00a0 Serum creatinine is 0.5 mg/dL and serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL.\u00a0 Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and negative blood.\u00a0 Multiple regions of the kidney are biopsied, and a representative image is shown below:</p><div class=\"mb-4 text-start flex justify-start items-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"w-full\"><button class=\"lg:max-w-lg md:max-w-md w-fit max-w-full my-5 hover:scale-[1.01] transition-[transform,_box-shadow] ease-in-out duration-500 hover:shadow-lg\"><img alt=\"\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-none\" src=\"../../Assets/images/hf5xypci4urhdxj150r1uw44.png\"/></button></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Acute interstitial nephritis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Crescentic glomerulonephritis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Membranous nephropathy"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Minimal change disease"}, {"label": "F", "text": "Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"}], "correctAnswer": "E", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_T43702\" style=\"overflow-x: auto; font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">M</mark>inimal change disease</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Epidemiology</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Highest prevalence in age 1-10</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pathogenesis</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">T cell\u2013mediated injury to podocytes</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Production of a glomerular permeability factor</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Clinical features</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Edema, frothy urine</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Diagnosis</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">LM: normal glomeruli</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">IM: no immune deposits</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">EM: diffuse podocyte foot process effacement</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">EM</strong> = electron microscopy; <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">IM</strong> = immunofluorescence microscopy; <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">LM</strong> = light microscopy.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient with edema, proteinuria, and hypoalbuminemia has <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-diy3nf92j8uvxbmz5mpsizrd\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-diy3nf92j8uvxbmz5mpsizrd\">nephrotic syndrome</a>.\u00a0 The <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-y971dgbi46hrh6vp72h5zim1\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-y971dgbi46hrh6vp72h5zim1\">normal glomeruli</a>\u00a0on light microscopy (LM) suggest a diagnosis of <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">minimal change disease</strong> (MCD), the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.\u00a0 MCD is often idiopathic but can also be triggered by medications such as <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</strong> (NSAIDs), which may be a contributing factor in this case; infection, allergy, or malignancy (eg, Hodgkin lymphoma) can also be associated.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Pathogenesis involves T-cell dysfunction causing overproduction of glomerular permeability factor (eg, IL-13).\u00a0 This cytokine damages podocytes and decreases the anionic charge of the glomerular basement membrane (GBM), resulting in selective proteinuria (ie, predominantly albumin).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Clinical features include acute weight gain, diffuse edema (eg, periorbital, pretibial), and frothy urine due to heavy proteinuria.\u00a0 Renal biopsy demonstrates <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">normal glomeruli on LM</strong>, with no immunoglobulin or complement deposits visible on immunofluorescent microscopy.\u00a0 However, electron microscopy shows diffuse <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-p9mxvtyeldkqayon6nr7gssl\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-p9mxvtyeldkqayon6nr7gssl\">effacement and fusion</a> of podocyte foot processes.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Acute <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-c7f5h5pciejt7mizgx9iikyl\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-c7f5h5pciejt7mizgx9iikyl\">interstitial nephritis</a> often occurs after initiation of new medications (eg, NSAIDs, diuretics) but causes acute kidney injury and white blood cell casts; heavy proteinuria is unexpected.\u00a0 Although the glomeruli are often normal, inflammation involving the interstitium and tubules is seen on LM.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choices B and F)</strong>\u00a0 Crescentic glomerulonephritis and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis cause nephritic syndrome (eg, hematuria, red blood cell casts, hypertension).\u00a0 <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ut3tn8lemp3oz3z1efpzngvm\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ut3tn8lemp3oz3z1efpzngvm\">Crescentic glomerulonephritis</a> occurs in multiple renal diseases (eg, Goodpasture syndrome, microscopic polyangiitis) and is characterized by hypercellular crescents composed of parietal and inflammatory cells.\u00a0 <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ydj9jqso6o3r0gy9k6t01z39\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ydj9jqso6o3r0gy9k6t01z39\">Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis</a>, which occurs 2-4 weeks after a group A <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Streptococcus</em> infection, demonstrates hypercellular glomeruli on LM.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-mkqrer0qnw91h012soq6vcz8\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-mkqrer0qnw91h012soq6vcz8\">Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis</a> causes nephrotic syndrome and also demonstrates similar podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy; however, LM demonstrates sclerotic foci within the glomerulus, which would be expected to appear given the multiple regions sampled in this patient.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ewua4a92x92jpympaunj5abk\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ewua4a92x92jpympaunj5abk\">Membranous nephropathy</a> causes nephrotic syndrome and is associated with NSAID use; however, this diagnosis is rare in children, and LM would show abnormal glomeruli with diffuse GBM thickening.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.\u00a0 It is often idiopathic but may be triggered by medications, infection, allergy, or malignancy.\u00a0 Light microscopy shows normal glomeruli, with no immunoglobulin or complement deposits on immunofluorescent staining.\u00a0 Electron microscopy shows diffuse podocyte foot process effacement and fusion.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.  It is often idiopathic but may be triggered by medications, infection, allergy, or malignancy.  Light microscopy shows normal glomeruli, with no immunoglobulin or complement deposits on immunofluorescent staining.  Electron microscopy shows diffuse podocyte foot process effacement and fusion.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c